  <\question>
    Let <math|\<cal-P\>=<around|{|p<rsub|1>,p<rsub|2>,p<rsub|3>,\<ldots\>|}>=<around|{|3,5,7,\<ldots\>|}>> be the set of odd prime numbers in increasing order. For fixed <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsub|\<geq\>2>> and <math|\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>>, define a sequence <math|<around*|{|X<rsub|n>|}><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|n=1>> as:

    <equation*|X<rsub|n>=<frac|1|k> <big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|k-1>p<rsub|n+i>+\<delta\>>

    Let <math|\<cal-A\>=<around|{|X<rsub|n>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>,n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|+>|}>> be the set of all integer terms of the sequence.

    Prove the following:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<delta\>=0> and <math|k = 2>, then <math|\<cal-A\>\<cap\>\<cal-P\>=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<delta\>=0> and <math|k> is odd, then <math|\<cal-A\>\<cap\>\<cal-P\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>.

      <item>For any <math|\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>> and <math|k\<gtr\>2> such that <math|\<cal-A\>> contains infinitely many odd integers, <math|\<cal-A\>\<cap\>\<cal-P\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </question>
</body>

